From 9282c0614f6f36ded97b25dc38d22aed48522532 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 20:56:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Put an A-Z strip above every library listing

Scrolling is the only way through a library listing today: it pages 50 at a time,
so the far end of the alphabet is dozens of loads away, and the search box matches
anywhere in a name so it cannot stand in for one.

Every library view gains a strip pinned under its toolbar - the whole alphabet
visible at once, led by '#' for the top of the listing, where the names that start
with a digit and the ones that reduce to nothing already sort. Picking a letter
asks the server to start the listing there.

Scrolling then works in both directions. Down needs nothing new: the letters after
the jump are still in the listing. Up asks for the mirror - the items before the
letter, with the ordering reversed so the nearest come first - and prepends each
page. Vuetify compensates the scroll position itself when a listing prepends, but
only while it is the scrolling element, and here the page scrolls instead, so this
holds the reader in place by hand.

The strip only appears while the listing is in name order. Sorted by date added or
play count, a letter would scatter matching items through the listing rather than
starting it somewhere, which is not what the strip promises.

What to ask the server for when walking up is a decision rather than wiring, so it
lives in helpers/alphabet_jump.ts where it is unit-tested. The wiring that calls it
is not: the listing's test harness does not render Vuetify's infinite scroller, so
a test reaching for it would assert nothing.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01VpS6zTXcRdworxLZ6Dp1De
